 {#s1f1}

We pre-registered participants and measured their performance. We randomly presented 90 pre-registered participants with only the summary information. Each participant received ten trials in total (five trials per condition). We then asked participants to describe what they had read.

Next we analyzed participants' comments. We re-formulated the task as a choice of 'rush', 'critical appraisal', 'neutral', or 'critical' response. An 'rush' response is a prompt to provide a brief summary, e.g., "The boat race!"

Coherent text
• Paragraph one provides an abstract (concept) as the subheading of the first paragraph, to justify the research questions;

• Paragraph two discusses the methods that will be used to answer the research questions;

• The rationale for choosing a qualitative design is provided in the paragraph (p2.3) of the abstract (p2.1) with the main concerns of the project;
